course: dictionary
lesson: 1825
title: Coalition: working together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A coalition is a group of people, parties or countries that join together to work towards one shared aim. Its root idea is joining or uniting, so think of a team that agrees to pull in the same direction.
explain: In politics, a coalition is a government made by two or more parties working together. This often happens when no single party wins enough seats in an election. «A coalition is when two or more parties share running the government.» Each party must agree to share the power and the work.
explain: Coalition can also mean a group of animals or friends who stick together. «Young males ... often wander with brothers or cousins in a group called a coalition.» The word is the same, but the group is a team, not a government. To check your meaning, ask: are these people or groups joining together for one shared aim?
fact: A coalition is two or more groups joining together for a shared aim.
fact: In politics, a coalition is a government made by two or more parties.
fact: Parties often form a coalition when no single party wins enough seats.
fact: «The Conservatives and Liberal Democrats» formed a coalition in the UK from 2010 to 2015.
fact: In Australia, «the Coalition» is the name for the Liberal and National parties working together.
exercise: «The Conservatives and Liberal Democrats formed a coalition in the UK from 2010 to 2015.» What is a coalition? | A type of old castle | *Two or more parties sharing the running of a government | A game played with a ball and two teams | A single party that wins every seat
exercise: «Young males ... often wander with brothers or cousins in a group called a coalition.» What kind of group is a coalition here? | A group of lions that wander together | *A group of animals that stick together for a shared aim | A group of tourists on holiday | A group of rivals that fight each other
exercise: Which sentence uses coalition in the political sense? | The pupils formed a coalition to plan the school fair | *The two parties agreed to share power in a coalition after the election | The wolves wandered as a coalition across the plains | My coalition of toys sits on the bed
test: «Yemen has had a war since 2014, backed by a coalition led by Saudi Arabia.» What does coalition mean here? | A kind of musical instrument | A quiet place to rest | *A group of countries joined together for a shared aim | A type of weapon
test: Ella says a coalition is just any sort of party. What is the best reply? | Yes, a coalition is a party with a big cake | *No, a coalition is a group of two or more parties or groups joined together for a shared aim | No, a coalition is always one single person | Yes, because it is a loud and happy gathering
exam: What do the words «two or more» tell you about a coalition? | It is always a country and never a school | It has no shared aim at all | *It needs at least two groups joining together | It needs exactly one leader only
